Here's the migration plan analysis:

ENDPOINTS & BEHAVIOR:
1. GET /health - Returns {"status": "ok"} (200)
2. GET /users/<user_id> - Returns user or 404 with {"error": "user_not_found"}
3. GET /products - Filterable by q (search) and category params, returns {"count": N, "items": [...]}
4. POST /login - Requires user_id in JSON body, sets session, returns 401 on failure
5. GET /me - Requires session, returns user or 401 errors
6. GET /admin - Requires admin role session, returns 403 for non-admins
7. GET /old-home - Redirects to /health (302)

SESSION MANAGEMENT:
- Uses Flask sessions with secret_key
- Stores user_id in session after login
- All auth checks verify session user_id exists in USERS

AUTHORIZATION:
- /me and /admin require authenticated session
- /admin additionally requires role="admin"
- Clear 401/403 error responses

DATA ACCESS:
- USERS dictionary (id -> user) with role field
- PRODUCTS list with filtering logic
- Currently all in-memory

MODULE RESPONSIBILITIES:
1. app.py - Flask app creation, config, and startup
2. routes.py - Route definitions and request handling
3. services.py - Business logic (user auth, product filtering)
4. repository.py - Data access (users/products CRUD)

MIGRATION STRATEGY:
- Keep same endpoint behaviors and status codes
- Move USERS/PRODUCTS to repository.py
- Move filtering/auth logic to services.py
- Keep session handling in routes
- Preserve all security checks
- Maintain same JSON response structures

